---
import Manuscript from '../../layouts/Manuscript.astro';
import {
  corpusOverview,
  corpusStats,
  constellations,
  nullModel,
  isolatedPredictionTest,
  crownExperiment,
  replicationAgreement,
  sequenceGrammar,
  motifIndex,
  textRecords,
  humanize,
  fmtInt,
  fmtPct,
} from '../../lib/atlas.js';

const corpus = corpusOverview();
const stats = corpusStats();
const index = motifIndex();
const constDoc = constellations();
const web = (constDoc.constellations ?? [])[0];
const nullDoc = nullModel();
const iso = isolatedPredictionTest();
const crown = crownExperiment();
const crownPrimary = (crown.results ?? []).find((r) => r.k === crown.primary_k) ?? (crown.results ?? [])[0];
const rep = replicationAgreement();
const seq = sequenceGrammar();

const pairs = (rep.pairwise ?? []).filter((p) => (p.shared_passages ?? 0) > 0);
const scores = pairs.map((p) => p.mean_canonical_jaccard).filter((v) => typeof v === 'number');
const agreeLo = scores.length ? Math.min(...scores) : null;
const agreeHi = scores.length ? Math.max(...scores) : null;
const sharedMax = Math.max(...pairs.map((p) => p.shared_passages ?? 0), 0);

const holdout = iso.holdout?.lineages ?? [];
const survivors = seq.survivors ?? [];
const dissolved = seq.strongPairCount - survivors.length;
const permTotal = iso.result?.permutations_beaten?.split('/')[1] ?? null;
---

<Manuscript
  title="What We Have Found — Ancient Wisdom Atlas"
  description="The project, its results so far, and what would prove it wrong — in plain language, with every number drawn live from the evidence indexes."
>
  <div class="folio">
    <section class="story-open" style="padding-bottom: 1rem;">
      <p class="rubric">Findings · in plain language</p>
      <h1>What we have found</h1>
      <div class="reading">
        <p class="footnote">
          Every number on this page is regenerated from the data indexes at build
          time. Nothing here is typed in by hand.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="asterism" />

    <section class="story-section">
      <div class="reading">
        <p class="rubric">The question</p>
        <h2>Strangers telling the same stories</h2>
        <p class="dropcap">
          People who never met — separated by oceans and thousands of years — told
          strangely similar stories. A hero descends into the land of the dead and
          returns. A great flood wipes the world clean. A mother goddess loses and
          finds her child. Why?
        </p>
        <p>
          There are three boring explanations: the stories were <em>inherited</em> from
          common ancestors, <em>traded</em> along contact routes, or the similarity is
          <em>coincidence</em>. And there is one profound possibility, suggested a
          century ago by Carl Jung and Joseph Campbell: that these patterns rise from
          something shared in the human mind itself. They could never test it. We can.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="asterism" />

    <section class="story-section gloss-grid">
      <div class="reading">
        <p class="rubric">What we built</p>
        <h2>An atlas with receipts</h2>
        <p class="dropcap">
          We collected {fmtInt(textRecords().length)} complete ancient and sacred texts
          from {fmtInt(corpus.traditionCount)} traditions — all public domain, all with
          provenance — and had AI read every passage and tag each recurring
          story-pattern (a <em>motif</em>) with the exact quote as evidence. Nothing is
          asserted without a passage you can click and <a href="/read/">read in the
          book</a>. So far: {fmtInt(index.motif_count)} distinct motifs across
          {' '}{fmtInt(index.occurrence_count)} tagged occurrences.
        </p>
        <p>
          Crucially, the corpus includes traditions that <strong>could not have
          borrowed from each other</strong> — Australian Aboriginal Dreamtime tales,
          Inuit stories from Greenland sledge journeys, San narratives from the
          Kalahari, Hopi and Zuni tellings, Guiana Amerindian legends. When the same
          pattern appears there <em>and</em> in Gilgamesh, “they copied it” is off the
          table.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gloss-col">
        <p class="gloss"><strong>{fmtInt(textRecords().length)}</strong> texts</p>
        <p class="gloss"><strong>{fmtInt(corpus.traditionCount)}</strong> traditions</p>
        <p class="gloss"><strong>{fmtInt(index.motif_count)}</strong> motifs</p>
        <p class="gloss"><strong>{fmtInt(index.occurrence_count)}</strong> evidence-anchored occurrences</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {pairs.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <hr class="asterism" />
        <section class="story-section gloss-grid">
          <div class="reading">
            <p class="rubric">Replication</p>
            <h2>Do different AIs see the same patterns?</h2>
            <p class="dropcap">
              We had three different AI models — built at different times, with no
              shared memory — each read the same ancient passages independently and tag
              the patterns they saw. If the patterns were imaginary, the three readers
              would disagree wildly. They don't.
            </p>
            <p>
              Across {fmtInt(sharedMax)} shared passages, any two readers agreed on
              {' '}{agreeLo !== null ? `${Math.round(agreeLo * 100)}–${Math.round(agreeHi * 100)}%` : '—'} of
              the pattern families they tagged (a strict overlap measure where 100%
              means identical answers). For open-ended reading tasks, that is the level
              of agreement trained human researchers reach.
            </p>
            <p>
              <em>Why it matters:</em> it means the patterns live in the texts
              themselves — not in one reader's imagination.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="gloss-col">
            <p class="gloss"><strong>3</strong> independent readers</p>
            <p class="gloss"><strong>{fmtInt(sharedMax)}</strong> shared passages</p>
            <p class="gloss"><strong>{agreeLo !== null ? `${Math.round(agreeLo * 100)}–${Math.round(agreeHi * 100)}%` : '—'}</strong> pairwise agreement</p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </>
    )}

    <hr class="asterism" />

    <section class="story-section">
      <div class="reading">
        <p class="rubric">Clusters</p>
        <h2>One web, not many</h2>
        <p class="dropcap">
          Jung claimed symbols travel in stable families. We joined motif families
          wherever they recur together across unrelated traditions, expecting
          archipelagos — a hero-cluster here, a creation-cluster there. Instead the
          {' '}{fmtInt(web?.internal_edges)} conserved edges close into a single
          connected constellation of {fmtInt(web?.size)} families. Across
          {' '}{fmtInt(nullDoc.config?.permutations)} shuffles, chance produced
          {' '}{fmtInt(Math.round(nullDoc.result?.null_mean))} conserved edges on
          average — never {fmtInt(nullDoc.result?.observed_conserved_edges)}
          {' '}(z = {nullDoc.result?.z_score}, p = {nullDoc.result?.p_value}).
        </p>
        <p class="footnote">
          The full web is drawn on <a href="/">the title page</a> and walkable from
          <a href="/descent/"> the descent</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="asterism" />

    <section class="story-section gloss-grid">
      <div class="reading">
        <p class="rubric">The preregistered test</p>
        <h2>The isolated lineages already knew</h2>
        <p class="dropcap">
          The sharpest test we have run — and we wrote the prediction down before
          looking. Learn the pattern-web from {fmtInt(iso.training?.traditions?.length)}
          connected Old World traditions ({fmtInt(iso.training?.records)} records,
          {' '}{fmtInt(iso.training?.conserved_edges)} conserved pairings), then open
          {' '}{fmtInt(holdout.length)} held-out lineages with little or no historical
          contact with that world — {holdout.slice(0, 6).map(humanize).join(', ')},
          and others.
        </p>
        <p>
          The isolated lineages reproduced {fmtPct(iso.result?.reproduction_rate)} of
          the trained web, where chance manages {fmtPct(iso.result?.null_mean)} and the
          luckiest of {permTotal ? fmtInt(Number(permTotal)) : 'all'} shuffles ever
          reached {fmtPct(iso.result?.null_max)}. The observed rate beat
          {' '}{iso.result?.permutations_beaten} permutations — a lift of
          ×{iso.result?.lift} over chance. The preregistered verdict:
          {' '}<span class="verdict">{iso.result?.verdict}</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Why it matters:</em> inheritance and trade cannot explain a pattern-web
          that re-forms among peoples who never touched it. Either the world's stories
          share deep structure, or chance got luckier than it managed in
          {' '}{permTotal ? fmtInt(Number(permTotal)) : 'many'} tries.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gloss-col">
        <p class="gloss"><strong>{fmtPct(iso.result?.reproduction_rate)}</strong> reproduced by isolated lineages</p>
        <p class="gloss"><strong>{fmtPct(iso.result?.null_mean)}</strong> expected by chance</p>
        <p class="gloss"><strong>{iso.result?.permutations_beaten}</strong> permutations beaten</p>
        <p class="gloss"><strong>×{iso.result?.lift}</strong> lift over the null</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="asterism" />

    <section class="story-section gloss-grid">
      <div class="reading">
        <p class="rubric">The capstone · assimilation-immune</p>
        <h2>Two worlds that never met, one architecture</h2>
        <p class="dropcap">
          The isolated-lineage test had one fair objection: to compare the isolated
          peoples with the connected world, we sorted their motifs into <em>our</em>
          families — so of course they looked familiar. The crown experiment removes
          that step entirely. We let the isolated world and the connected world each
          build their own motif-families from scratch, blind, never seeing each other
          or our categories — then asked whether the two webs of “which patterns
          travel together” have the same shape.
        </p>
        <p>
          At {crown.primary_k} families each,
          {' '}<strong>{fmtPct(crownPrimary?.reproduction)}</strong> of the isolated
          world's bonds reappear in the connected world's, where chance yields only
          {' '}<strong>{fmtPct(crownPrimary?.null_mean)}</strong>. The match beat
          {' '}{crownPrimary?.permutations_beaten} permutations
          (p = {crownPrimary?.p_value}), and a second, label-free test found the two
          webs' structures statistically indistinguishable (degree-KS p =
          {' '}{crownPrimary?.degree_ks_p?.toFixed(2)}). The preregistered verdict:
          {' '}<span class="verdict">{crown.verdict}</span>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Why it matters:</em> no mapping into shared categories can explain a
          match between two webs that were never allowed to see one another. It is
          convergence, not identity — nearly half of each world remains its own — but
          it is more agreement than chance can reach. The full walkthrough, the
          numbers, and the honest limits are on <a href="/crown/">the Crown</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gloss-col">
        <p class="gloss"><strong>{fmtPct(crownPrimary?.reproduction)}</strong> bonds in both worlds</p>
        <p class="gloss"><strong>{fmtPct(crownPrimary?.null_mean)}</strong> expected by chance</p>
        <p class="gloss"><strong>{crownPrimary?.permutations_beaten}</strong> permutations beaten</p>
        <p class="gloss"><strong>{crownPrimary?.degree_ks_p?.toFixed(2)}</strong> topology-KS p (indistinguishable)</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="asterism" />

    <section class="story-section">
      <div class="reading">
        <p class="rubric">Order · a falsification we keep on the shelf</p>
        <h2>Campbell's grammar mostly wasn't</h2>
        <p class="dropcap">
          Campbell claimed stories don't just share ingredients — they share a
          sequence: departure, ordeal, return. We tested it.
          {' '}{fmtInt(seq.strongPairCount)} strong before-and-after orderings emerged
          from the texts, and under within-text permutation {fmtInt(dissolved)} of them
          dissolved — chance writes those orderings on its own. As a universal grammar,
          the hypothesis failed; {fmtInt(survivors.length)} lines of order held. We
          publish the falsification beside the findings, because that is the point.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="asterism" />

    <section class="story-section">
      <div class="reading">
        <p class="rubric">What would prove us wrong</p>
        <h2>The exit conditions</h2>
        <p class="dropcap">
          If the patterns only appeared in cultures that had contact; if independent AI
          readers stopped agreeing when texts got unfamiliar; if the “universal”
          patterns failed the chance test — the profound explanation would lose, and we
          would say so. One of our own favorite hypotheses already has. The full
          apparatus — agreement matrices, permutation counts, per-lineage tables — is
          laid out in <a href="/lab/">the Lab</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Manuscript>
